  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nEarnest shower and thunderstorm development is underway across\nthe Ohio River Valley out ahead of a cold front slated to pass\noverhead tonight into Sunday morning. An unstable airmass\npreceding the front has yielded optimal conditions for cumulus\nto quickly develop further into scattered showers and storms.\nWith destabilization quickly arising, a Severe Thunderstorm\nWatch has been issued in collaboration with the Storm Prediction\nCenter and neighboring offices. This Watch currently encompasses\nportions of southeast Ohio and north-central West Virginia,\nwhere severe weather may be observed within the next few hours.\nAdditional expansion of the Watch is not out of the question for\nthe southern half of the forecast area, but will continue to\nmonitor the evolution of storms to determine how the rest of the\nafternoon and evening will shake out.\n\nStorms preceding the front today will be capable of producing\ndamaging wind gusts, hail, and heavy downpours that may lead to\nflash flooding. While some soil recovery has occurred in\nlocations who recently faced a multitude of days featuring\nflooding, additional rainfall could invigorate further hydro impacts\nacross the region. Storms will be progressive in nature, which\nshould help to alleviate a response from instantaneous rainfall\nrates, but locations would then be susceptible to any training\neffects that may set up through the course of the evening.\n\nConvection marches into the post-sunset timeframe, but will\ngradually diminish with the progression of the front overnight.\nPrecipitation will then bank up along the northeast WV mountains\nby early Sunday morning and wrap up altogether by daybreak once\nthe front has completed its passage through the mountains. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, Sunday will feature the start of\ndry weather that stretches into the start of next week. High\npressure settling into the Ohio Valley will yield a push of dry\nair, alongside much cooler temperatures. Daytime highs will span\nthe 60s along the mountains and 70s to low 80s in the lower \nelevations, while overnight conditions will feature low \ntemperatures tumbling down into the 50s and 60s. Next\nopportunity for precipitation arrives for the second half of the\nwork week, when a warming trend instigates diurnally driven\nshowers and storms over the Central Appalachians.",
